{
  "_comment": [
    "The running clock's rule (LIVESTACK-48): what the clock shows at a moment of the raw video.",
    "Read by tests/shared/test_pass_clock_rule.py, which checks the Python rule",
    "(shared/pass_clock.py) and the JavaScript one (review_ui/static/js/pass_clock.js)",
    "against every case. text null = no clock shown (before the largada).",
    "Several cases are float traps: 0.3 * 10 is 2.9999999999999996 and 1.0 - 0.9 is",
    "0.09999999999999998 in floating point, so a naive floor would show one step too few.",
    "LIVESTACK-53: result_shown = is the result pill shown at that moment. With a clock,",
    "the result appears when the clock freezes (and stays to the end of the clip); before the",
    "largada, and while it runs, it is not shown.",
    "late_cases: does the clip end (cut_out_s) before the clock stops? Then the result never",
    "appears and the job page says so. A freeze exactly at the cut-out is too late: the clip has",
    "no frame at or after its cut-out.",
    "LIVESTACK-67: speed_cases = is the top speed under the clock shown at time_s? It is shown from",
    "the speed-trap mark (speed_mark_s, raw-video seconds) to the end of the clip, by the largada's",
    "rule: whole microseconds, each time rounded half up on its own, shown when at or after the mark.",
    "Read by tests/shared/test_speed_rule.py (Python and JavaScript) like the cases above.",
    "LIVESTACK-68: speed_before_end_cases = where the speed starts when the prova shows it for the",
    "last N seconds of the clip (speed.seconds_before_end). The clip ends at cut_out_s, or at the",
    "video's end (duration_s) without a cut-out; start_s = that end minus N, in whole microseconds",
    "rounded down to the millisecond, never before the cut-in (a null cut-in is 0). A null start_s",
    "means no end is known, so no speed. Read by tests/shared/test_speed_before_end_rule.py."
  ],
